<!--
  Benchmark Mode Configuration Example

  Add this <benchmark> element inside your <configs> root element in configs.xml

  Usage:
    1. Enable benchmark mode and set frame count
    2. Build and launch Skyrim
    3. Manually load your benchmark save file
    4. Physics will run for N frames, then auto-exit
    5. Check hdtSMP64.log for timing statistics

  Attributes:
    - enabled: Enable/disable benchmark mode
    - save: Save file name (currently unused - load manually)
    - frames: Number of frames to capture (10-10000)
    - exit-when-done: Auto-exit after completion
    - suppress-ui: Hide HUD during benchmark (not yet implemented)
    - quiet-mode: Disable audio (not yet implemented)
-->

<benchmark
  enabled="false"
  save=""
  frames="2000"
  exit-when-done="true"
  suppress-ui="true"
  quiet-mode="false"
/>

<!--
  Example workflow:

  1. Create benchmark save:
     - Load Skyrim normally
     - Position yourself with desired NPCs/entities
     - Save as "benchmark_12entities.ess"

  2. Enable benchmark in configs.xml:
     <benchmark enabled="true" frames="2000" />

  3. Run via Python script:
     python scripts/benchmark_pipeline.py run -name test1 -frames 2000

  4. OR run manually:
     - Launch Skyrim
     - Load your benchmark save
     - Stand still
     - After 2000 frames, game auto-exits

  5. Results in hdtSMP64.log:
     [BENCHMARK] Complete: 2000 frames processed
     Timings over 1000 frames:
       CPU:
         Internal update mean 527.930 us, std 139.971 us
         Collision launch mean 835 us, std 335.604 us
         ...

  6. Tracy CSV automatically exported to results/<name>/trace.csv

  7. Ingest to MCP for semantic queries:
     The pipeline script automatically calls mcp__hdt-log__ingest_tracy
-->
